WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.840
For most of the history of science, the molecules of living things were thought to

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.040
need living things to make them.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.500
Sugar, fat, urea: all of it was supposed to carry a vital force.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.100
The chemist Berzelius drew the line.

5
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.860
Inorganic matter, the chemistry of rocks and salts, obeyed ordinary law.

6
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.400
Organic matter, the chemistry of living bodies, did not.

7
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:39.633
In February 1828, Friedrich Wöhler warmed an inorganic salt, ammonium cyanate.

8
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:46.133
What crystallized in his flask was urea, the molecule the body makes to dispose of

9
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.793
nitrogen. There was no kidney in the room.

10
00:00:49.913 --> 00:00:56.493
Both compounds have the same formula: one carbon, four hydrogens, two nitrogens, one

11
00:00:56.493 --> 00:00:59.753
oxygen. Same atoms, connected differently.

12
00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:02.673
Heat rearranged the connections.

13
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:07.653
Wöhler's flask did not kill vitalism that afternoon.

14
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:13.753
His critics had an honest objection: ammonium cyanate could be traced back to

15
00:01:13.753 --> 00:01:19.473
organic sources. The vital force pulled back, but it had somewhere to stand.

16
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:27.573
In 1845 Hermann Kolbe built acetic acid from carbon disulfide, made from charcoal

17
00:01:27.573 --> 00:01:30.953
and molten sulfur, with nothing alive in the chain.

18
00:01:30.953 --> 00:01:37.153
A decade later Marcellin Berthelot built fats from glycerol and fatty acids.

19
00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:39.893
One redoubt remained.

20
00:01:40.993 --> 00:01:45.173
Louis Pasteur had proved that fermentation depends on living yeast.

21
00:01:46.153 --> 00:01:51.513
From this he drew a stronger line than his data required: that fermentation was

22
00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:54.293
inseparable from the intact living cell.

23
00:01:55.313 --> 00:01:57.793
That conviction held for forty years.

24
00:01:58.813 --> 00:02:06.153
In 1877 Wilhelm Kühne proposed the word enzyme, from the Greek for in leaven, for the

25
00:02:06.153 --> 00:02:08.033
soluble agent inside yeast.

26
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:16.087
In 1896 Eduard Buchner ground brewer's yeast with sand and pressed out a clear

27
00:02:16.087 --> 00:02:20.027
golden juice with almost no intact cells in it.

28
00:02:20.307 --> 00:02:22.227
He added sugar to preserve it.

29
00:02:22.747 --> 00:02:28.227
The sugar began to ferment, carbon dioxide rising through a flask of dead yeast.

30
00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:32.127
Vitalists said living scraps must have survived.

31
00:02:33.087 --> 00:02:34.727
Buchner ran two controls.

32
00:02:35.687 --> 00:02:39.627
Boiled juice did not ferment, so the agent was a destructible chemical.

33
00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:43.607
Filtered juice still did, so no cells were needed.

34
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:51.660
Seventy years of syntheses made the vital force unnecessary, one molecule and one process

35
00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:58.240
at a time. What replaced it is the premise of this course: a living cell obeys the

36
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.740
same chemistry as everything else, with no separate law of its own.

37
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.160
The catalysts Kühne named and Buchner freed are ordinary proteins.
